+# Natural ARM rewrite of 08000430
+
+Tags: #08000430 #arm #matching #natural-c
+
+Exploratory solo work on `codex/arm-cx1`, following `build/arm_kit/BRIEF.md`.
+Started from `build/arm_kit/drafts/clean_c1.c` and compared the Boktai 2 assembly
+and video C references. No register bindings, dummy initializers, compiler
+behavior overrides, artificial statement splits, full builds, or commits.
+
+The best whole-function candidate is
+`build/arm_kit/drafts/best_natural.c`: 89 differing normalized instructions,
+89.1% sequence similarity (+16.5 percentage points over the supplied baseline),
+96.6% register-blind shape similarity (+6.7 points). This is a nonmatching
+experiment, with no exact coverage gained. It is not installed in `src/`.
+The incoming modified source was restored byte-for-byte.
+
+Useful changes: delay tile-source access until reservation succeeds by passing
+an object/source field to the queue helper; represent the fixed EWRAM request
+buffer as a memory-map pointer; compose the OAM word in one assignment.
+The extern-array alternative is retained in `best_symbolic.c` (106 diffs).
+The cursor hoist plus independent reservation-test address reload arises
+naturally, but its preheader schedule still differs. The remaining large
+structural issue is Boktai 3's separately materialized request base versus the
+indexed constant address produced by the natural draft and seen in Boktai 2.
+
+The architecture is strongly supported: parallel collection arrays, four
+affine indices, signed branch/shift rounding, palette deduplication, and two
+emit loops. A negative-error tile helper and a fixed-buffer accessor are
+plausible original-source forms; their precise interfaces remain tentative.
+Ordinary signed division, boolean flip state, broader emit helpers, and
+halfword descriptor stores all regressed.
+
+Validation: default comparison and uninstrumented production compiler agree;
+20,000 host-side randomized state comparisons against the starting draft pass.
+This does not establish equivalence against the ROM or verify the GBA ABI.
+All 58 numbered trials and limitations are in `build/arm_kit/REPORT.md`;
+per-trial drafts and compiler output remain local under the ignored kit.
+
+## Follow-up 1: natural request-base and cursor lifetimes
+
+Tags: #08000430 #arm #matching #natural-c
+
+Explored 60 additional natural variants from `best_natural.c`, in the requested
+order: B3 request-base construction, palette/affine allocation, cursor-preheader
+scheduling. No candidate improved the best: still 89.1% sequence similarity
+(+0.0 percentage points this follow-up), 89 normalized instruction differences.
+Production compiler reconfirmed this result. The incoming source was restored.
+
+Natural early buffer handles can produce base-first request indexing, but the
+base remains a long-lived register or stack value. The clearest structural
+candidate is `build/arm_kit/drafts/base_first_111.c` (111 diffs). In the
+function-scope example, sched1 splits the queue initializer into two sets
+before local allocation; its constant REG_EQUAL note does not become a
+constant REG_EQUIV, and the pointer spills. EWRAM layout structs, typed
+accessors, mutating pointer parameters, live palette-pointer reuse and
+whole-record appends do not recover the ROM's in-loop rematerialization.
+
+Fixed-point macros, sprite bitfields and palette-search abstractions do not
+improve the affine/palette region. Explicit cursor handles worsen allocation;
+direct cursor accessors and parameterized helpers fold back to the existing
+best and leave its preheader schedule unresolved. There is no support for
+promoting the dead initializer into a supposedly natural early live initializer.
+The missing short-lived base copy remains a source-recovery question.
+
+Detailed trial table and diagnostics are appended to `build/arm_kit/REPORT.md`;
+all variants remain in `build/arm_kit/drafts/f1_*.c`. No exact match, integration,
+commit, push or full build was performed.
